-- title:
--   Hecke operators on J₀(N) commute (all levels)
-- statement:
--   For every natural number $N$ with $N \neq 0$, the project's predicate [`ModularCurve.HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar N`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L25) holds; unfolding its definition, this says that for every pair of primes $\ell, \ell'$ (elements of `Nat.Primes`, so including primes dividing $N$) the endomorphisms `heckeOperatorBar N ℓ` and `heckeOperatorBar N ℓ'` commute as elements of the ring $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{Z}}(\texttt{JZero } N)$, i.e. $T_\ell \circ T_{\ell'} = T_{\ell'} \circ T_\ell$. Here `JZero N` is the group $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of degree-zero divisor classes of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$ for all divisors $d \mid N$ (a function-field model of $X_0(N)$), and `heckeOperatorBar N ℓ` is the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map underlying `heckeOperatorAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) N ℓ`. The latter is a total term defined by a case split: when the package `HeckeInputsAlong` of divisor-theoretic inputs at level $N\ell$ is available (integrality of the two degeneracy maps $\alpha$ (inclusion) and $\beta$ ($q \mapsto q^\ell$), existence of principal divisors at level $N\ell$, finiteness along $\alpha$, the fundamental identity along $\beta$ and the norm formula for pushforward along $\alpha$), it is the correspondence $\alpha_* \circ \beta^*$ on $\operatorname{Pic}^0$; otherwise it is the zero map. The assertion is therefore commutativity of these closed terms, with no hypotheses beyond $N \neq 0$.
--
--   Classically, the Hecke correspondences on $X_0(N)$ commute, whence the Hecke algebra acts commutatively on the Jacobian; see Diamond–Shurman §5.2 or Mazur's treatment of the divisorial operators. The formal statement differs from the textbook one in being about the total terms `heckeOperatorBar N ℓ`, which coincide with $\alpha_*\beta^*$ precisely when the divisor-theoretic inputs at level $N\ell$ hold, and in treating all primes uniformly, so that the operators attached to $\ell \mid N$ (the $U_\ell$ of the classical notation) are included. Its role in the project is to discharge the commutativity hypothesis on which `heckeEvalBar` and the module structure `heckeModuleBar N` depend, so that `heckeModuleBar N` is the genuine action of `HeckeAlg` on `JZero N` rather than the fallback action; it is consequently cited throughout the Hecke-module arc, in the Čerednik–Drinfeld specialisation statements, in the cohomological carriers of Ihara-type arguments, and in the construction of eigenvectors in Tate modules of $J_0(N)$.
